1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:06,771
Welcome to chapter sixteen of my study series on Build Your First LLM by Hasan Degismez.

2
00:00:06,771 --> 00:00:20,035
Today we turn a working notebook into software other people can use: a versioned config, a FastAPI service, JSON lines logging, metrics, input validation, rate limiting and tests.

3
00:00:20,035 --> 00:00:31,818
I ran the notebook, and then I ran the book's complete app against a real local Llama 3.2 through Ollama, with all four of the book's tests plus two of my own.

4
00:00:31,818 --> 00:00:36,190
One of mine found a false positive in the injection filter.

5
00:00:37,040 --> 00:00:41,170
Chapter fifteen gave us an app that works when we're watching.

6
00:00:41,170 --> 00:00:46,099
Today we make it reliable for other people, and next chapter we deploy it.

7
00:00:46,099 --> 00:00:51,760
The chapter sixteen notebook uses a mock chat function, so it runs without any model.

8
00:00:52,610 --> 00:00:53,303
The plan.

9
00:00:53,303 --> 00:00:55,382
What changes in production.

10
00:00:55,382 --> 00:00:56,152
Packaging.

11
00:00:56,152 --> 00:00:57,770
Serving with FastAPI.

12
00:00:57,770 --> 00:00:59,079
Logs and metrics.

13
00:00:59,079 --> 00:01:01,158
Validation and rate limits.

14
00:01:01,158 --> 00:01:01,774
Testing.

15
00:01:01,774 --> 00:01:06,086
Then the complete app, which I ran against a real model.

16
00:01:06,086 --> 00:01:07,010
Then review.

17
00:01:07,860 --> 00:01:08,606
Key terms.

18
00:01:08,606 --> 00:01:13,302
Production is code running for real users without you watching.

19
00:01:13,302 --> 00:01:15,688
An endpoint is a URL and method.

20
00:01:15,688 --> 00:01:18,968
A health check reports whether you're alive.

21
00:01:18,968 --> 00:01:21,876
Rate limiting caps requests per client.

22
00:01:21,876 --> 00:01:25,231
Graceful degradation means failing helpfully.

23
00:01:25,231 --> 00:01:28,511
And JSON lines logs are one object per line.

24
00:01:29,361 --> 00:01:29,937
Part one.

25
00:01:29,937 --> 00:01:31,089
A different world.

26
00:01:31,939 --> 00:01:39,096
In development you are the only user, errors are lessons, you debug with print and you can rerun a cell.

27
00:01:39,096 --> 00:01:49,624
In production many people use it at once, errors mean frustrated users, you debug by reading last night's logs, and the system has to recover on its own.

28
00:01:50,474 --> 00:01:52,841
The book lists the classic failures.

29
00:01:52,841 --> 00:02:00,794
A huge prompt, a sudden crowd, a prompt injection, a failure at night with no logs, and a fix that breaks something else.

30
00:02:00,794 --> 00:02:02,832
The principle: fail gracefully.

31
00:02:02,832 --> 00:02:08,288
Don't crash, tell the user something useful, log it and keep serving everyone else.

32
00:02:09,138 --> 00:02:09,719
Part two.

33
00:02:09,719 --> 00:02:11,591
What exactly are we shipping?

34
00:02:12,441 --> 00:02:19,551
For an Ollama based app, the model is really the model tag plus your system prompt and settings.

35
00:02:19,551 --> 00:02:24,143
The book saves them as a versioned JSON file with a timestamp.

36
00:02:24,143 --> 00:02:29,550
My run wrote config version one point zero point zero exactly as printed.

37
00:02:30,400 --> 00:02:32,066
Use semantic versioning.

38
00:02:32,066 --> 00:02:39,907
A bug fix bumps the patch number, a compatible feature the minor number, and a behaviour change the major number.

39
00:02:39,907 --> 00:02:48,235
My opinion: changing the system prompt or the model is a behaviour change, so version it like code and keep a changelog.

40
00:02:49,085 --> 00:02:53,145
Why does a three billion parameter model fit on a laptop?

41
00:02:53,145 --> 00:02:54,071
Quantization.

42
00:02:54,071 --> 00:03:01,409
The copy I use is a four bit build of about two gigabytes, against about six point four in sixteen bit.

43
00:03:01,409 --> 00:03:05,042
You lose a little quality and gain a lot of memory.

44
00:03:05,892 --> 00:03:06,848
Part three.

45
00:03:06,848 --> 00:03:09,455
Making it reachable over HTTP.

46
00:03:10,305 --> 00:03:11,681
HTTP in one slide.

47
00:03:11,681 --> 00:03:13,669
An endpoint plus a method.

48
00:03:13,669 --> 00:03:15,733
Get reads, post sends data.

49
00:03:15,733 --> 00:03:29,419
Every response has a status code: two hundred OK, four hundred bad request, four twenty two failed the schema, four twenty nine too many requests, and five hundred means we broke.

50
00:03:30,269 --> 00:03:32,258
Here's the minimal service.

51
00:03:32,258 --> 00:03:41,756
A Pydantic request model with a validator that rejects empty messages, a response model, and a chat endpoint that times the call.

52
00:03:41,756 --> 00:03:47,720
FastAPI validates both directions automatically and generates docs at slash docs.

53
00:03:48,570 --> 00:03:51,983
A good health check also checks its dependencies.

54
00:03:51,983 --> 00:03:55,397
The book's version calls the model with "say OK".

55
00:03:55,397 --> 00:04:02,084
My caution: that's a full generation every time a monitor polls, which can be every few seconds.

56
00:04:02,084 --> 00:04:05,566
On my CPU one generation took over twenty seconds.

57
00:04:05,566 --> 00:04:10,373
Cheaper: ask Ollama for its model list, and cache the result briefly.

58
00:04:11,223 --> 00:04:18,113
Run it with uvicorn, then call it with curl, with Python requests, or from the interactive docs page.

59
00:04:18,963 --> 00:04:21,671
Here's my redrawing of the request path.

60
00:04:21,671 --> 00:04:35,614
Each stage can stop a bad request with its own status code: the rate limiter with four twenty nine, Pydantic with four twenty two, our validator with four hundred, and a failed generation with five hundred.

61
00:04:35,614 --> 00:04:37,780
Everything is logged at the end.

62
00:04:38,629 --> 00:04:39,257
Part four.

63
00:04:39,257 --> 00:04:40,763
Knowing what it's doing.

64
00:04:41,613 --> 00:04:43,578
Logs are a flight recorder.

65
00:04:43,578 --> 00:04:51,146
The book logs metadata only: lengths, latency, success and errors, never the prompt itself, for privacy.

66
00:04:51,146 --> 00:05:00,898
Here's a real line from my run: a twenty eight character prompt, a four character answer, twenty six seconds of latency on a busy CPU.

67
00:05:01,748 --> 00:05:09,070
For metrics, a dictionary is enough to start: requests, errors and latency, exposed at slash metrics.

68
00:05:09,070 --> 00:05:12,259
It resets on restart and counts per process.

69
00:05:12,259 --> 00:05:16,609
Real dashboards add percentiles, because averages hide pain.

70
00:05:16,609 --> 00:05:23,060
One thirty second request in a hundred barely moves the mean, but P ninety nine shows it.

71
00:05:23,910 --> 00:05:30,393
LLMs generate one token per forward pass, so a hundred tokens means a hundred passes.

72
00:05:30,393 --> 00:05:34,359
The book estimates five to fifteen seconds on a CPU.

73
00:05:34,359 --> 00:05:39,241
On my busy eight core box a short reply took twenty six seconds.

74
00:05:39,241 --> 00:05:44,198
So set generous timeouts, show progress, and stream when you can.

75
00:05:45,048 --> 00:05:45,886
Part five.

76
00:05:45,886 --> 00:05:47,309
Expect bad input.

77
00:05:48,159 --> 00:05:57,617
The validator rejects long inputs and a few injection phrases with regular expressions, returns a generic error and logs the attempt.

78
00:05:57,617 --> 00:06:06,933
One small inconsistency: the comment says the limit is in tokens, roughly, but the code compares string length, so it's characters.

79
00:06:07,783 --> 00:06:09,780
Here's what my own test found.

80
00:06:09,780 --> 00:06:15,104
The complete app shortens one pattern to just "act as", with no word boundaries.

81
00:06:15,104 --> 00:06:18,632
I sent: in fact as a beginner, how do I learn Python?

82
00:06:18,632 --> 00:06:24,489
It was rejected with four hundred, because the letters "act as" appear inside "fact as".

83
00:06:24,489 --> 00:06:27,751
Contact as soon as possible would be blocked too.

84
00:06:27,751 --> 00:06:32,743
Add word boundaries, and remember keyword filters only stop casual attacks.

85
00:06:33,593 --> 00:06:41,033
The rate limiter keeps a list of recent timestamps per IP and returns four twenty nine past twenty a minute.

86
00:06:41,033 --> 00:06:42,479
My test confirmed it.

87
00:06:42,479 --> 00:06:43,306
Two caveats.

88
00:06:43,306 --> 00:06:45,579
It lives in one process's memory.

89
00:06:45,579 --> 00:06:56,739
And behind a proxy or a cloud platform, the client host may be the proxy's address, so every user shares one limit unless you read the forwarded header carefully.

90
00:06:57,589 --> 00:06:58,408
Part six.

91
00:06:58,408 --> 00:06:59,317
Insurance.

92
00:07:00,167 --> 00:07:04,951
FastAPI's test client calls your app in process, no server needed.

93
00:07:04,951 --> 00:07:12,924
Test the happy path, but also the error paths: empty input gets four twenty two, long input gets four hundred.

94
00:07:12,924 --> 00:07:17,853
Add unicode, the rate limit and what happens when the model is down.

95
00:07:18,703 --> 00:07:19,473
My results.

96
00:07:19,473 --> 00:07:22,346
The notebook's three mock tests all pass.

97
00:07:22,346 --> 00:07:30,263
Against the real model, the book's four tests pass, including the long input test, which the notebook leaves out.

98
00:07:30,263 --> 00:07:32,084
My rate limit test passes.

99
00:07:32,084 --> 00:07:36,708
My false positive test fails, which is exactly what tests are for.

100
00:07:37,558 --> 00:07:44,437
Canary deployments send a new version to a small slice of traffic first, then ramp up or roll back.

101
00:07:44,437 --> 00:07:52,984
The book says chapter seventeen builds the infrastructure for this, but chapter seventeen only uses Modal's rolling deploy.

102
00:07:52,984 --> 00:07:54,582
There's no canary step.

103
00:07:55,432 --> 00:07:57,699
Graceful degradation in practice.

104
00:07:57,699 --> 00:08:03,402
Give every model call a timeout, so a stuck generation can't hold a worker forever.

105
00:08:03,402 --> 00:08:08,692
On timeout return a five oh three with a friendly message, not a stack trace.

106
00:08:08,692 --> 00:08:12,745
Optionally fall back to a smaller model or a cached answer.

107
00:08:12,745 --> 00:08:16,936
Keep the user message generic and put the details in the log.

108
00:08:17,786 --> 00:08:19,063
One thing I'd add.

109
00:08:19,063 --> 00:08:27,077
Read settings like the model name, rate limit and any API key from environment variables, never from code in git.

110
00:08:27,077 --> 00:08:38,778
The book's chapter seventeen refers back to chapter sixteen's environment variable and API key pattern, but chapter sixteen doesn't actually cover it, so here it is.

111
00:08:39,628 --> 00:08:40,349
Part seven.

112
00:08:40,349 --> 00:08:41,463
The complete app.

113
00:08:42,313 --> 00:08:49,500
The complete app puts it all together: config, logger, rate limiter, validator, models and three endpoints.

114
00:08:49,500 --> 00:08:54,000
Chat goes rate limit, validate, call the model, then log and count.

115
00:08:54,000 --> 00:09:00,851
And around it all sits the production loop: detect, diagnose, fix with a regression test, and monitor.

116
00:09:01,701 --> 00:09:03,117
Small fixes I'd make.

117
00:09:03,117 --> 00:09:04,196
Word boundaries.

118
00:09:04,196 --> 00:09:05,611
A cheap health check.

119
00:09:05,611 --> 00:09:12,825
Count rejected requests in the metrics too, because right now validation failures never reach the counters.

120
00:09:12,825 --> 00:09:14,982
Say characters, or count tokens.

121
00:09:14,982 --> 00:09:20,442
Use the forwarded header for the rate limit key, but only from a proxy you trust.

122
00:09:20,442 --> 00:09:25,296
And for the bonus cache, include the model and system prompt in the key.

123
00:09:26,146 --> 00:09:28,102
My checklist before sharing.

124
00:09:28,102 --> 00:09:31,106
Tests are green, including the error paths.

125
00:09:31,106 --> 00:09:36,205
Logs exist, you can read them, and they hold no prompts or personal data.

126
00:09:36,205 --> 00:09:39,138
Health is cheap and metrics count rejects.

127
00:09:39,138 --> 00:09:43,190
Every limit is set: length, rate, max tokens and timeouts.

128
00:09:43,190 --> 00:09:47,032
And the version in slash health matches your changelog.

129
00:09:47,881 --> 00:09:48,690
Part eight.

130
00:09:48,690 --> 00:09:49,204
Review.

131
00:09:50,054 --> 00:09:51,002
Short answers.

132
00:09:51,002 --> 00:09:54,859
Development is you, production is many users without you.

133
00:09:54,859 --> 00:09:58,987
Health proves liveness and should check dependencies cheaply.

134
00:09:58,987 --> 00:10:00,746
Log metadata, not content.

135
00:10:00,746 --> 00:10:03,859
Rate limiting protects capacity and your bill.

136
00:10:03,859 --> 00:10:08,529
And test error paths, because that's where production actually lives.

137
00:10:09,379 --> 00:10:19,121
The checkpoint: health and chat endpoints, validation with word boundaries, content free logs, a metrics endpoint and at least four tests.

138
00:10:19,121 --> 00:10:21,027
Add my false positive test.

139
00:10:21,027 --> 00:10:24,909
The bonus is a five minute cache for identical prompts.

140
00:10:25,759 --> 00:10:29,815
Practice: run the complete app against Ollama with pytest.

141
00:10:29,815 --> 00:10:35,969
Add my false positive test, watch it fail, fix the regular expression and watch it pass.

142
00:10:35,969 --> 00:10:42,122
Then plot latency from your log and compare the median with the ninety ninth percentile.

143
